<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Emotional and Self-Development in Adolescence</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00058</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>eba8705c-e36c-4630-92df-d3b00bae91da</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Describe the course of emotional development during adolescence</item>
    <item>Discuss the development of self-regulation and emotional intelligence in adolescence</item>
    <item>Describe the development of the self-concept and self-esteem in adolescence</item>
    <item>Explain the relevance of body image in adolescence</item>
    </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">The alarm sounds early on 15-year-old Haoyu’s school days. After dressing and eating breakfast, he practices 20 minutes of meditation each morning. Meditation helps clear his mind and focus his energy and emotions. Today he is especially stressed because he has unit exams in two classes and a paper due in another. As he enters school, Haoyu joins friends exchanging fist bumps. During lunch, two of his tablemates start teasing the girl sitting alone at the end of the table. Haoyu weighs saying something to them or moving to a different table to avoid trouble. He decides to move. The end of the school day brings a sense of relief, although several hours of homework await. Once home, he grabs a seaweed snack and retreat to his room to scroll through social media for a bit on his phone. Haoyu’s day is not only busy but full of different motivational states, opportunities for self-regulation, and emotions.</para>
    <para id="para-00003">An Emotions are the result of a complex set of cognitive, physiological, behavioral, and environmental components and interactions (Ekman, 1972, Scherer, 1997). They often motivate our behavior, so it’s not surprising that they play a prominent role in adolescent experience. Let’s explore the role of emotions as teens develop an understanding of themselves, including their developing emotional intelligence, self-concept, and self-esteem, and their increased ability to self-regulate. We’ll also consider body image and its impact the developing teen’s mental health.</para>
    <section id="sect-00002">
    <title>Interpersonal and Socioemotional Health Risks</title>
    <para id="para-00004">Teen interpersonal health risks often stem from their socioemotional needs for dating and establishing romantic relationships with appropriate peers. Romantic relationships are crucial for socioemotional development because they help us develop intimacy skills and shape our identity and autonomy (Connolly &amp; McIsaac, 2009). However, they can also generate intense emotions and conflicts, sometimes with the potential for teen dating violence (TDV).</para>
    <para id="para-00005">The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2024) defines dating violence as a variety of unsafe behaviors inflicted on a partner. Psychological dating violence includes psychologically or publicly manipulative actions aimed at indirectly harming the partner. Physical dating violence can range from pushing and slapping to more severe acts like beating, kicking, strangling, or burning. Sexual TDV includes forced sexual activities, from unwanted touching to forced penetration. Stalking is a pattern of repeated, unwanted attention and contact by a current or former partner that causes fear or safety concerns for an individual or someone close to them (CDC, 2024).</para>
    <para id="para-00006">Many adolescents report experiencing violence in their romantic relationships. A meta-analysis by Wincentak et al. (2017) found that 20% of teens aged 13 to 18 have experienced physical dating violence, and 9% have experienced sexual dating violence. Given how common dating violence is, and the severity of its effects including psychological distress, suicidal thoughts, and post-traumatic symptoms (Exner-Cortens et al., 2013; Hébert et al., 2017), it is now seen as a serious public health problem.</para>
    <para id="para-00007">Dating violence and sexual assault are profound and multifaceted. Victimized teens may engage in risky behaviors like substance abuse or develop eating disorders as coping mechanisms (Batchelder et al., 2022). Studies also indicate that TDV is longitudinally associated with increasing high-risk behaviors, such as marijuana and alcohol use, and poor mental health outcomes, particularly for those who experience victimization (Piolanti et al., 2023). Trauma can also impair their academic performance and social relationships, leading to isolation (Cava et al., 2020) and further emotional distress. Addressing these risks requires comprehensive education, supportive services, and accessible resources to ensure teens can establish and maintain healthy, respectful relationships. For instance, the reality of TDV should be included in school curricula focused on sex and relationship education. Schools must commit to fostering a violence-free social environment and encourage bystander intervention (Banyard et al., 2020).</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Emotional Intelligence</title>
    <para id="para-00008">For many adolescents, the social world expands significantly. Not only do they spend more time outside the home, both in school and at after-school activities, but their new reasoning capabilities, fueled by physical brain developments, make interacting with peers and non-family adults a central means of development. They learn to recognize and understand their own emotions, including their triggers, strengths, and weaknesses, and they begin achieving self-awareness. They learn to control impulses, manage stress, and adapt to changing circumstances with the developing skill of self-regulation. They are on their way to achieving <term id="term-00001">emotional intelligence (EI)</term>, the ability to recognize our own and others’ emotional states and feelings, to regulate our emotions, and to remain motivated. (Goleman, 1998).</para>
    <para id="para-00009">Emotionally intelligent individuals can also express their emotions, convey empathy, and maintain their psychological, social, and emotional well-being (Alina et al., 2021). Through social interactions adolescents learn to express their emotions in appropriate verbal and nonverbal ways. This communication skill in turn helps them grow increasingly sophisticated at resolving conflicts and establishing healthy boundaries in their relationships. And emotional intelligence allows the adolescent to use emotions as valuable information for problem-solving with rationality and emotional agility.</para>
    <para id="para-00010">Parents can also help foster emotional intelligence. In particular, the authoritative parenting style has shown a positive relationship with the development of emotional intelligence; the authoritarian style has been linked with lower emotional intelligence in adolescents (Nastas &amp; Sala, 2012; Nguyen et al., 2020). Recall that authoritative parents are sensitive to their child’s daily needs and emotional states, actively encourage them to express themselves, and help them work through emotional, motivational, and social challenges. These behaviors model the use of emotional intelligence (Reyes-Wapano, 2021). In non-Western societies, authoritarian parenting is common, and children are expected to follow rules and decisions without explanation or feedback. Asian and African students often exhibit lower self-esteem and self-concept, but they tend to benefit more from this parenting style in their academic performance and psychological adjustment.</para>
    <para id="para-00011">Emotional intelligence also has long-lasting benefits. Adolescents with high EI have stronger academic performance, are less likely to experience school burnout, have a deeper sense of life satisfaction overall, and tend to view themselves as having the coping skills and capabilities needed to overcome adversity (Collado-Soler, et al., 2022). Next, we’ll look at self-regulation more specifically.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Self-Regulation</title>
    <para id="para-00012">Recall the major developments in the frontal lobe and limbic system, discussed in <link document="m00051"/>. These support the adolescent’s growing ability to exert <term id="term-00002">self-regulation</term>, the ability to manage and control our own behavior and emotions without outside assistance (Muraven &amp; Baumeister, 2000; McClelland et al., 2018). This skill allows us to change or inhibit certain thoughts, emotions, and behaviors to achieve better outcomes (Baumeister &amp; Alquist, 2009; Moilanen et al., 2015). Related abilities include task persistence, delayed gratification, self-monitoring, self-reward for progress, management of frustration and distress, and the capacity to seek help when needed (Murray &amp; Rosanbalm, 2017) (<link target-id="fig-00001"/>). For example, it might be fun to slap your fist on condiment packets during lunchtime at school, but a self-regulating teenager may refrain from doing so in order to avoid detention or the loss of privileges (like an upcoming class trip).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00001">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!--<image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_10_02_Athlete.jpg"/>-->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>(a) Climate activist Greta Thunberg and (b) advocate for female education Malala Yousafzai became activists as teens and have demonstrated persistence in those activities into adulthood.</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00013">Self-regulation increases steadily across adolescence, shaped in part by parental and peer influences (Farley &amp; Kim-Spoon, 2014). Parents regulate a child’s behavior and provide an initial set of expectations and guidance. As an adolescent develops and exhibits self-control, explicit parental regulation can be reduced. A good-quality parent-adolescent relationship predicts good self-regulatory skills in the developing teen. However, this effect can work both ways, and a teen having difficulty regulating their emotions or behaviors will likely contribute to a difficult parent-teen relationship.</para>
    <para id="para-00014">Positive peer relationships are also an important predictor for, and outcome of, good self-regulation skills, including emotional self-regulation. This influence too goes both ways. Teens who exhibit strong self-regulation skills have been found to form caring, close friendships with peers. In turn, these close peer relations can help to support and reinforce self-regulation (Scholte &amp; Van Aken, 2020; Meldrum &amp; Hay, 2012).</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00005">
    <title>Self-Concept and Self-Esteem</title>
    <para id="para-00015">Our self-concept is the mental image or representation we have of ourselves, the cognitive portion of identity. During adolescence, it goes beyond the tangible descriptions of middle childhood (like “baseball fan” or “middle child”) to encompass abstract qualities like “fair” and “loyal.” Teens develop this understanding by a self-comparison with others’ characteristics and abilities (Harter, 2006). For example, a 15-year-old might pride herself on being “trustworthy” and “hard working.” She judges herself to be as trustworthy as her best friend; she knows she works far harder than most peers in her classes.</para>
    <para id="para-00016">The dimensions of the self-concept, as outlined by Harter (1983), are scholastic competence, social acceptance, athletic competence, physical appearance, behavioral conduct, and self-acceptance. By the end of adolescence, they have been built into a coherent whole that informs an overall identity. Research that has studied children longitudinally suggests self-concept is relatively stable from middle childhood to middle adolescence (Kuzucu, et al., 2014), which may be a result of peer perception and niche-picking (Chapter 2). For example, once a 10-year-old demonstrates athletic prowess, peers will likely continue to react to this fact, reinforcing this part of the self-concept (peer perception). Likewise, someone who discovers they are good at a skill or activity will likely seek situations that support it (niche-picking).</para>
    <para id="para-00017">If self-concept is the cognitive understanding of our self, <term id="term-00003">self-esteem</term> is the motivational and emotional piece (Harter &amp; Whitesell, 2003). Numerous studies have found a link between exhibiting low self-esteem and developing depression (Masselink et al., 2017; Nelis &amp; Bukowski, 2019). Adolescents with low self-esteem have been found to have a smaller group of friends who can offer social support, and to show social adjustment problems (Marshall et al., 2014).</para>
    <para id="para-00018">Overall, however, self-esteem rises over the course of adolescence, and the factors most likely to predict it at a later age include positive parental relationships, healthy physical activity, and positive body image (Birkeland et al., 2012). No clear sex differences in average levels of self-esteem have been reported (Masselink et al., 2017). Healthy self-esteem and a sense of mastery can be enhanced by practicing strategies like mindfulness and self-compassion (Marshall et al., 2015). Positive reinforcement, supportive feedback, and encouragement of self-discovery and physical activity can also help.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Body Image</title>
    <para id="para-00019">Physical appearance is one of the main dimensions of self-concept. Our mental image or representation of our body is our <term id="term-00004">body image</term> <link target-id="fig-00002"/> and is closely related to self-esteem. Given the rapid physical development of adolescence, and the social environment’s ever-changing response to them, body image becomes a major psychological feature of the adolescent experience. If someone’s body image is healthy, their mental representation of their body size and shape is healthy and positive. An unhealthy body image is just the opposite; the person’s view of their body does not match objective reality and contains negative self-evaluations (Engeln, 2017; Voelker et al., 2015).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00002">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!--<image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_10_02_BodyImage1.jpg"/>-->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>[Caption placeholder]</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00020">All adolescents are susceptible to the development of an unhealthy body image. The timing of puberty is influential here because cultural ideals for physical beauty can heighten a teen’s sense of being on time or off-track for physical development relative to peers and cultural expectations. For early-maturing adolescent females in the United States, for example, breast and hip development differs from the current beauty standard of a thin or athletic body shape (Lee, et al., 2007). In fact, for both adolescent males and females, early maturation is linked to a variety of negative psychological, social, and health outcomes (Hoyt et al., 2020). In a recent study of 574 Chinese teenagers aged 8-15, girls were generally more unhappy with their gender and looks, while boys were more unhappy with their sexual organs. Girls felt more dissatisfaction with their sexual organs before their breasts started developing and with their gender after they started their periods (Zhang et al., 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00021">Research on teen body image highlights the interplay among media exposure, cultural values, and individual factors shaping body image perceptions in different ethnic groups. For instance, among Latina girls, frequent viewing of mainstream television is linked to decreased body satisfaction, while Black-oriented television improves body satisfaction for more acculturated Latina girls (Schooler, 2008). Black adolescent girls’ exposure to stereotypical media images correlates with negative appraisals, moderated by racial identity, indicating that media portrayal significantly affects body image (Jean et al., 2022). Asian American women’s body image perceptions respond to pressures from both mainstream White culture and Asian culture. For Asian American youth, family and societal messages contribute to body image perceptions, which can lead to disordered eating and treatment-seeking behaviors (Javier et al., 2019).. [Table]</para>
    <para id="para-00022">Information that contributes to adolescents’ body image comes from peers, family members, television and movies, advertisements, and social media. The Surgeon General of the United States recently published an advisory for parents, educators, and practitioners warning of the dangers of social media use by adolescents (U.S. Surgeon General, 2023). It notes a particular concern for adolescent girls, citing an increase in the risk for developing body image and eating disorders (Holland &amp; Tiggemann, 2016). Parents’ words, attitudes, and behaviors related to health, diet, exercise, and physical development contribute to teens’ sense of their physical selves, and mothers play a significant role in their teenage daughters’ views (Helfert &amp; Warschburger, 2011; Neumark-Sztainer, et al., 2010) <link target-id="fig-00003"/>. Peers provide feedback about which body types are valued and how someone is doing relative to those standards.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
    <para id="para-00023">This video offers <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104advertise">a glimpse into what goes on behind the camera</link> to create an actual beauty advertisement. A CBS News program <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104socmedteen">shows that social media is fueling a movement among teens to change their looks</link> to meet unrealistic beauty standards.</para>
    </note>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00002">
    <para id="para-00024">Watch this video to learn about <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104ELondon">a school in East London being the first to start lessons using a Body Confidence Campaign Toolkit</link> to help schoolchildren develop healthy confidence in their bodies.</para>
    </note>
    <figure id="fig-00003">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!--<image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_10_02_Healthy.jpg"/>-->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>[Caption placeholder]</caption>
    </figure>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00003">
    <para id="para-00025">Watch this clip of <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104nonjudge">actors depicting a non-judgmental parent-teen conversation about body positivity</link>, followed by some tips about such conversations from a nutritionist:</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00026">What can we do to encourage a healthy body image in adolescents? Many approaches can work. (Mostafavi, 2022). Modeling self-love is a great starting point. Parents with healthy body images model healthy behaviors, emotions, and thoughts for their children. Similarly, adults can embrace and encourage body diversity, the idea that people come in all shapes and sizes and all have different strengths. Further, teaching children about media literacy and critically analyzing media messages builds a good set of lifelong skills, helping children counteract negative information about beauty ideals as represented in the media. Finally, one counter-intuitive but important recommendation is to avoid saying positive things about someone based solely on notions of physical beauty. By praising someone for their physicality alone, adults are inadvertently signaling that this matters above other characteristics like personality, intelligence, or talents. Instead, praise desirable characteristics and effort.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00004">
    <para id="para-00027">Watch the TED talk by Kylie Miller to learn about <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104bodyimage">separating body image from performance</link>. Societal pressure suggests that individuals achieve a specific body standard for success in activities, which can negatively impact adolescent mental health. Having participated in ballet and gymnastics throughout her life, Miller has witnessed firsthand the toxic impact of such activities on youths’ body image. Through this talk, she aims to raise awareness of these social issues and inspire a shift in how adolescent activities are perceived.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section class="summary" id="sect-00007">
    <list id="list-00002">
    <item>Adolescence is a crucial time for developing emotional intelligence (EI), an ability that includes self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy, and social skills. High EI in adolescents correlates with better academic performance, life satisfaction, and coping skills.</item>
    <item>Brain development during adolescence supports self-regulation, allowing teens to manage and control their behavior and emotions and to develop task persistence, delay gratification, and manage frustration.</item>
    <item>During adolescence, the self-concept grows to encompass abstract traits like “trustworthy” and “hardworking.” Positive self-concept in teens is linked to higher self-esteem, better mental health, and more successful social relationships.</item>
    <item>Self-concept is the cognitive understanding of our self, while self-esteem is the motivational and emotional piece. Self-esteem is influenced by parental support, peer relationships, physical activity levels, and body image. It tends to increase across adolescence, and no sex differences have been reported.</item>
    <item>All adolescents are susceptible to developing an unhealthy body image, influenced by the timing of puberty and cultural beauty standards. The development of a positive body image is a critical goal.</item>
    </list>
    </section>
